The RR-MoA routing overhead ($+3.1$\,ms) is dominated by the sparse expert selection loop; the router itself (Conv1d + linear, ${\sim}1.1$K params) adds $<0.1$\,ms. SR-MoA dense and Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} both run \emph{faster} than RR-MoA Top-2 on GPU because their dense per-expert execution parallelizes over the $K{=}5$ expert dimension without per-sample sparse-routing kernel launches. Notably, Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} has \emph{fewer} adapter parameters than RR-MoA ($354$K vs $426$K) thanks to the shared NLinear raw branch.

At deployment scale, swapping a per-tenant adapter loads orders of magnitude fewer parameters than reloading the backbone, so a shared-backbone topology scales by adapter hot-swap rather than by replication. We do not claim a memory advantage over per-tenant DLinear: a shared TSFM with many small adapters is larger in aggregate than the same number of DLinears. The deployment case for TSFMs is instead cross-task reuse, zero-shot onboarding, microsecond adapter hot-swap, and the elimination of $O(N)$ per-tenant training pipelines (see \S\ref{sec:intro} and Appendix~\ref{app:deployment}).

